The question then occurs: How is this different from spoilers?

The spoilers are generated by looking at the code.  But, due to
cosmological accident, everyone who read that code may have misread it
in the same way!  Thus, the spoilers can be different than what
actually happens.  (And, I'd like to point out, this isn't all that
uncommon occurance.  It is, after all, this disjoint between what we
read and what is that generates those bugs we so know and love)

At a certain number of samples, the confidence I'd assign in game
derived knowledge would exceed that I'd assign that derived from
reading the code.

According to the code, it may say that priests can be of any alignment.
But, if a player never sees a chaotic priest after a few hundred
games, I would be suspecting the understanding of the code was wrong.

http://groups-beta.google.com/group/rec.games.roguelike.nethack/msg/dabf61f1fe8cf3d1

Thus, I would say the whole "You can never really know the truth from
experimentation" is invalid.  You can never really know the truth from
inspecting the source either.  In both cases, there is a probability
that you have got an incorrect answer.
